+(durations)=
+
+### Durations
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string such as `1h30m` to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `90`, or one or more parts with the units `d`, `h`, `m`, `s`, and `ms`, in that order, such as
+`2d4h` or `1m500ms`. Each unit may appear at most once, and there are no separators.
+
+`min` and `max` restrict the duration to an inclusive range, in the same way as {class}`IntRange`. Negative durations are
+rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    from datetime import timedelta
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--wait", type=click.Duration(min=timedelta(seconds=5), max=timedelta(hours=1)))
+    def cli(wait):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {wait.total_seconds()} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(cli, args=['--wait=1m30s'])
+    invoke(cli, args=['--wait=2h'])
+```
+
